<p class="study-intro">
    A Words of Plainness Companion Essay
</p>

<div class="study-scripture-block">
    God who commanded light to shine out of darkness has filled our hearts with that same light in witness of His Son, Jesus Christ. Yet we bear this treasure as fragile clay&mdash;earthen vessels, flawed and breakable, yet entrusted with eternal worth. The treasure is not the vessel. The vessel cracks, but the light is not put out. We are pressed, but not forsaken; struck down, but not destroyed. For the excellency of the power is of God, and not of us.<br>
    <span style="display:block; text-align:right; font-style:normal; opacity:0.85; margin-top:0.75rem;">&mdash;Paraphrasing <a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/nt/1-cor/1?lang=eng&id=p25-p29#p25" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1 Corinthians 1:25–29</a>, <a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/nt/2-cor/4?lang=eng&id=p6-p8#p6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2 Corinthians 4:6–8</a>, <a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/nt/rom/3?lang=eng&id=p23#p23" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Romans 3:23</a></span>
</div>

<section class="content-section" id="the-wrenching-season">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2 class="study-section-heading">The Wrenching Season</h2>
        <p class="study-prose">Many believers pass through a season of wrenching together, though few of us would have chosen it, and fewer still know quite what to call it. These are troubled times in which flaws and cracks in the institutions and leaders we trusted feel exposed in painful ways. Communities once held up as sanctuaries have been shaken by scandal, betrayal, and disillusionment. Some of us have lost faith in particular leaders. Some have lost faith in entire traditions. Many have not lost faith in Christ Himself, and yet have found ourselves wondering how the gospel survives when the vessels carrying it crack so visibly and so often.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Such periods are as the proverbial Dark Night of the Soul, but for the masses rather than the individual. Believers are now experiencing such a season, and it is not the first time. Every century of Christianity has produced its own wrenching. The shape of the pain is recognizable across the generations: the institution one trusted has cracked, the leader one followed has fallen, the certainty one rested upon has been shaken. The names and the dates change. The shape does not.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The pastoral question, then, is not the question many of us instinctively ask first. The first question, when grief is fresh, is <em>which institutions have failed?</em> That question can be answered, and the answer matters. But it is not the deepest question, and it cannot bear the weight of the gospel's reply. The deeper question, the one this essay attends to, is something else: <em>what does the gospel say about a faith carried by failed instruments, and by instruments that only looked failed to those watching?</em> The gospel has an answer. The answer is older than the wrenching. And the answer is meant to set the believer free to grieve honestly, and to keep walking.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<div class="study-divider">&#10022;&ensp;&ensp;&#10022;&ensp;&ensp;&#10022;</div>

<section class="content-section alt" id="pattern-older-than-headlines">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2 class="study-section-heading">The Pattern Is Older Than the Headlines</h2>
        <p class="study-prose">The biblical record, read honestly, never pretended its leaders were unbroken. Read slowly, it shows four kinds of broken vessel: those who cracked and were restored, those who cracked and were not, those who were judged cracked by people who could not see, and a people who walked a commanded road imperfectly. Each kind teaches something the others cannot.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">What follows sorts the scriptural accounts into four kinds. As you read each account, try naming its kind yourself before the essay does&mdash;and keep the four in hand for the cases Scripture does not cover.</p>
        <h3 class="study-subsection-heading">Cracked and Restored</h3>
        <p class="study-prose">Moses, the primary lawgiver of Israel, provides the earliest archetype of the &ldquo;ill-tempered shepherd.&rdquo; His ministry did not begin until after he killed an Egyptian, hid the body in the sand, and fled into forty years of exile (Exodus 2:11&ndash;15). When God called him from the burning bush, Moses argued, hesitated, and pled inadequacy until the Lord's anger was kindled against him (Exodus 3&ndash;4). Across his ministry, Moses failed at the waters of Meribah, struck the rock in anger when commanded to speak to it, and was barred from entering the promised land for that single act of disobedience (Numbers 20:7&ndash;13). And yet Moses is the man through whom the law came; the man whose face shone from speaking with God; the man who stood with the Lord on the Mount of Transfiguration. The vessel was cracked. The treasure was not.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The story of King David represents a most profound collision between divine favor and human brokenness. Described as &ldquo;a man after God's own heart,&rdquo; he was a figure of extreme contradictions&mdash;a feared warrior who composed exquisite Psalms, a beloved king who committed adultery and arranged murder. The list of his moral and personal failures includes adultery and the murder of Uriah, the abuse of royal power, deception of Joab, willful disobedience in the census of Israel, and pride in his military strength. He failed catastrophically in his role as a husband and father; his polygamous household produced rivalry, violence, and rebellion among his children. And yet the prophets named him as the type of the Messiah; the Psalter is largely his work; the line of Christ runs through him. Scripture does not whitewash his failures. Scripture refuses to.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Aaron, the first high priest of Israel, cracked in another way. While Moses was on the mount, the people demanded gods they could see, and Aaron gave them one. He gathered their gold, fashioned the calf, built an altar before it, and proclaimed a feast (Exodus 32:1&ndash;6). When Moses came down and confronted him, Aaron blamed the people, and then the fire: &ldquo;I cast it into the fire, and there came out this calf&rdquo; (Exodus 32:24). It is the oldest institutional excuse in the record&mdash;the office yielding to the crowd, and the officer deflecting what he had done. And yet Aaron was not cast off. He remained high priest, and the priesthood of Israel passed through his line.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">There are more examples. Jonah disobeyed his prophet calling, fleeing from God's command (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/ot/jonah/1?lang=eng" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jonah 1</a>). Then he bitterly resented God's mercy toward Nineveh (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/ot/jonah/4?lang=eng" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jonah 4</a>), revealing bigotry and a narrow heart. Peter denied his Lord three times (Matthew 26:69&ndash;75) and was publicly rebuked by Paul for hypocrisy and racial favoritism in Antioch (Galatians 2:11&ndash;14). Paul himself consented to the stoning of Stephen and harried the early church before his conversion (Acts 8:1&ndash;3), and afterward called himself the chief of sinners (1 Timothy 1:15). The disciples bickered about who among them would be greatest (Luke 22:24). The apostles sometimes misunderstood Christ's plainest teaching. The book that gives us the gospel gives us, in the same hand, the unflattering record of every leader through whom that gospel came.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The cumulative effect, when one walks slowly through these stories, is that Scripture itself never pretended otherwise. There is no New Testament epistle written to a perfect church. There is no Old Testament prophet whose ministry escaped public correction. The pattern is too consistent to be accidental. The Holy Spirit, in directing the canonical record, seems to have wanted us to know that the message is not the messenger.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The pattern continues into the scriptures of the Restoration. <a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/dc-testament/dc/3?lang=eng" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Doctrine and Covenants 3</a> records the Lord's rebuke of Joseph Smith for the loss of the 116 pages of the Book of Mormon manuscript. He is rebuked by name, in the canonized record, for what the Lord calls his fearing &ldquo;man more than God.&rdquo; D&amp;C 5 and D&amp;C 93 contain further rebukes. The pattern of the Restoration's own canon is the pattern of the biblical canon: the prophet is real, his calling is real, and his failures are real, all named together in the same authoritative text.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Before being called to his prophetic calling, Alma the Elder was a corrupt priest and official who participated in a tyrannical religious-political structure that extorted the people and was known for &ldquo;much priestcraft and abominations&rdquo; (Mosiah 11). Following his conversion he became a great prophet and the founder of the church among the Nephites. His conversion came in a courtroom, and we will return to it. The pattern is not foreign to Restoration scripture; it is its native form.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">His son, Alma the Younger, went about with the sons of Mosiah seeking to destroy the church until an angel stopped him (Mosiah 27:8&ndash;11). Alma's own son Corianton forsook his mission to follow a harlot, and his father told him plainly what it had cost (Alma 39:3&ndash;4). Yet Corianton was restored, and returned to preaching the word (Alma 49:30). Three generations of one family, each cracked, each carried back into the work.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Other examples of this same pattern of broken leaders in the Book of Mormon include prophets, apostles, kings, judges, and disciples who made human mistakes and fell to temptations, often serious enough to be named explicitly in the record. The Book of Mormon's editorial voice&mdash;Mormon, Moroni, Nephi, and the others who shaped the abridgment&mdash;refused to sanitize the people they were writing about. They named the failures because the failures were part of the witness. The witness was not that the prophets were unbroken; the witness was that God spoke through broken prophets and worked through broken peoples to bring about the salvation of His children.</p>
        <h3 class="study-subsection-heading">Cracked and Not Restored</h3>
        <p class="study-prose">Not every cracked vessel is restored, and the record does not pretend otherwise. Saul was anointed king by the prophet's own hand, and lost the kingdom for a disobedience he would not own (1 Samuel 15:22&ndash;23). Solomon built the temple of the Lord, and when he was old his heart turned after other gods (1 Kings 11:1&ndash;11). Balaam spoke true words from God and loved the wages of unrighteousness (Numbers 31:16; 2 Peter 2:15). Judas was chosen by Christ Himself, carried the bag, and betrayed his Lord with a kiss (John 12:4&ndash;6; Matthew 26:47&ndash;50). Laman and Lemuel saw an angel with their own eyes, and never fully turned (1 Nephi 3:29&ndash;31).</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The Restoration states the principle plainly. When we use a calling &ldquo;to cover our sins, or to gratify our pride, our vain ambition, or to exercise control or dominion or compulsion upon the souls of the children of men, in any degree of unrighteousness, behold, the heavens withdraw themselves; the Spirit of the Lord is grieved; and when it is withdrawn, Amen to the priesthood or the authority of that man&rdquo; (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/dc-testament/dc/121?lang=eng&id=p37#p37" target="_blank" rel="noopener">D&amp;C 121:37</a>).</p>
        <p class="study-prose">A calling does not protect the one called. The call is an invitation to be shaped, and the shaping requires the vessel's continuing willingness. The addendum traces how that willingness works across a lifetime, and across the life of a church. Here it is enough to say that God's choosing never takes away the vessel's choosing.</p>
        <h3 class="study-subsection-heading">Judged Cracked by the Watching World</h3>
        <p class="study-prose">There is a third kind, and it is the easiest to misjudge. Some vessels looked cracked to everyone watching, and were not.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Abraham told Pharaoh, and later Abimelech, that Sarah was his sister. To the courts of Egypt and Gerar it was a foul deception, and it brought misery on many. Pharaoh's house was plagued for it (Genesis 12:17), and the wombs of Abimelech's household were closed (Genesis 20:18). Abimelech's protest is the court's verdict in its own words: &ldquo;thou hast done deeds unto me that ought not to be done&rdquo; (Genesis 20:9). Restoration scripture records what the court could not see: the Lord had told Abraham to say it (Abraham 2:22&ndash;25). And in the very chapter of the deception, God tells Abimelech who this man is: &ldquo;he is a prophet, and he shall pray for thee&rdquo; (Genesis 20:7). It is the first time Scripture calls anyone a prophet.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Hosea was commanded to take &ldquo;a wife of whoredoms&rdquo; (Hosea 1:2), a marriage every respectable neighbor would have judged a disgrace. It became the living parable of God's faithfulness to an unfaithful people.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Nephi was constrained by the Spirit to slay Laban, and he records his own resistance: &ldquo;I shrunk and would that I might not slay him&rdquo; (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/bofm/1-ne/4?lang=eng&id=p10#p10" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1 Nephi 4:10</a>). He gives the Spirit's reason in the Spirit's words&mdash;&ldquo;It is better that one man should perish than that a nation should dwindle and perish in unbelief&rdquo; (1 Nephi 4:13)&mdash;and then he obeys. He does not argue for himself. He leaves the account to stand.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Alma, watching the believers of Ammonihah cast into the fire, was constrained by the Spirit not to stretch forth his hand to save them (Alma 14:10&ndash;11). It was obedience that looked to his companion, and would look to any onlooker, like a failure to act.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The Lord has no obligation to excuse Himself to mankind. Scripture does not try to excuse Him either. It records what He commanded, records how it looked to those watching, and lets both stand. That is a truth about God, and not a privilege any mortal leader may borrow; the discipline of this doctrine, below, draws that line plainly.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Other vessels were judged cracked for nothing but their faithfulness. Jeremiah was seized and condemned to death for prophesying against the city, and was spared (Jeremiah 26). Peter and John were arrested and threatened before the council, and were let go (Acts 4&ndash;5). Stephen was not spared; he was stoned while praying for those who stoned him (Acts 7:59&ndash;60). Christ Himself, the one vessel without a crack, was condemned by the high priest as a blasphemer (Matthew 26:65). Some escaped the evil judgment of their peers, and some did not.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The prophet Abinadi did not. He was bound and tried before King Noah and his priests for testifying &ldquo;that God himself should come down among the children of men&rdquo; (Mosiah 17:8), and he sealed the truth of his words by his death (Mosiah 17:20). But one of the priests in that court &ldquo;believed the words which Abinadi had spoken&rdquo; (Mosiah 17:2). His name was Alma, and the corrupt priest became the prophet we met above. In one courtroom the faithful vessel was condemned and the cracked vessel was converted. The judgment of the watching world is not the judgment of God. An accusation is not a verdict, in either direction, and the discipline below names how to tell the difference.</p>
        <h3 class="study-subsection-heading">A Commanded Road, Walked Imperfectly</h3>
        <p class="study-prose">The fourth kind is a people rather than a person. Israel was led out of Egypt by the hand of God, on a road He commanded, and murmured against Him at nearly every turn of it; a whole generation fell in the wilderness short of the land it had been promised (Numbers 14:26&ndash;35). The command was God's. The walking was theirs, faithful and faithless by turns.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The Restoration records the same pattern, and the Lord names it Himself. The early Saints were commanded to build up Zion, and they gave much to obey. Yet when they were driven from their lands, the Lord told them why: &ldquo;there were jarrings, and contentions, and envyings, and strifes, and lustful and covetous desires among them&rdquo; (D&amp;C 101:6). Later He added a sentence that carries the thesis of this whole essay: Zion might have been redeemed even then, &ldquo;were it not for the transgressions of my people, speaking concerning the church and not individuals&rdquo; (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/dc-testament/dc/105?lang=eng&id=p2#p2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">D&amp;C 105:2</a>). The Lord Himself diagnosed the institution. A people can walk a road God commanded with marvelous dedication and sacrifice, and still walk it mingled with the pride and moral failings common to all mankind.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The Restoration's own scripture refuses religious hero-worship as firmly as the Bible does. The Lord chastens His prophets in real time, in their own sacred records, with their names attached. He does so because the gospel is not protected by pretending its instruments are perfect. The gospel is protected by the truth that its power is not the instruments' power. The cracks in the vessels are part of the testimony, not against it.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Behind every one of these stories stands a single Pauline sentence, brief enough to memorize and weighty enough to carry the entire pattern: <em>We have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency of the power may be of God, and not of us</em> (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/nt/2-cor/4?lang=eng&id=p7#p7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2 Corinthians 4:7</a>). Paul is not describing an unfortunate accident the gospel has had to work around. He is describing the gospel's design.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<div class="study-divider">&#10022;&ensp;&ensp;&#10022;&ensp;&ensp;&#10022;</div>

<section class="content-section" id="no-polity-exempt">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2 class="study-section-heading">No Vessel Spared, No Polity Exempt</h2>
        <p class="study-prose">The Lord's own diagnosis of His church points beyond the canon. If the pattern is universal in scripture, it is also universal in the world the scriptures are addressing. Every form of Christian church government, every tradition, every era, has carried the pattern in its own shape. The cracks are not the failure of one church or one century. The cracks are the human condition meeting the institution-form, and they appear wherever both are present.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The signature crack in hierarchical traditions&mdash;those built on episcopal succession and the concentration of authority in offices that descend from the apostolic age&mdash;has been the temptation to protect the institution's reputation by hiding what would damage it. The Roman Catholic clerical sexual abuse crisis is the most documented example, but the pattern is not unique to the Roman Church. The hierarchical signature crack does not require Roman polity. It requires only the structural temptation to subordinate accountability to office.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The signature crack in connectional traditions&mdash;those built on layered conferences, presbyteries, and synods&mdash;has been the temptation to defer accountability through procedural complexity, so that the harm one body sees is referred to another body that lacks the standing to act. The Episcopal Church's Title IV process documents this precisely; the United Methodist Church and the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America walk the same arc in the same shape, for the same reasons.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The signature crack in congregational traditions&mdash;those built on the autonomy of the local church and the gathering around a called teacher&mdash;has been the temptation to grant a charismatic founder unchecked authority, on the reasoning that the gathering is the gift, and to question the founder is to risk the gathering. The Southern Baptist Convention sexual abuse reckoning, the 2025 Robert Morris case, and the 1980s televangelist scandals all document this same pattern in its own shape.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The signature crack in decentralized movements&mdash;those built on networks rather than offices, where the gospel travels through relationships rather than through chains of ordination&mdash;has been the temptation to substitute local autonomy for genuine accountability, so that no one is finally answerable for what the network at large has done. The M&uuml;nster Rebellion of 1534 stands as the historical archetype; the 2023&ndash;2024 reckoning at the International House of Prayer Kansas City stands as the contemporary echo. Both share the same structural architecture: the prophetic mantle claimed in immediate revelation, defended by armed loyalty or institutional momentum, becomes the only authority the network recognizes&mdash;and the very weakness of structural accountability that the polity celebrates as gospel-shaped mobility produces the absence of any check on a single charismatic founder's worst impulses.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Restorationist traditions face a temptation that is the mirror image of their gift&mdash;the gift of restored prophetic authority, the temptation to use the weight of the mantle of authority to either suppress what conscience would speak or to cover what should be exposed, in order to protect the name of the Lord's Church from shame. The Utah Reformation rhetoric and the Mountain Meadows Massacre stand as the most documented case from inside our own tradition; the Jehovah's Witness two-witness rule and the September Six disciplinary actions of 1993 walk the same pattern in different dimensions. The reckoning, where it has come, has been costly and partial. The cracking continues; so does the reckoning.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Each polity has its signature crack. None has escaped its own. The pastoral question is not whether this is true&mdash;every honest historian inside every tradition will tell you it is&mdash;but what the gospel does in the face of the pattern. And what the gospel does, demonstrably, has been to keep working.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The pattern is not an accident, and it is not a shortcoming the gospel has had to work around. God has chosen to carry the gospel in fragile clay rather than in flawless gold, and the next section asks why.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The gospel principle of action is this: no disciple, in any tradition, may safely make the institution the foundation of their faith. What shall we do?</p>
        <p class="study-prose">Build the household of your faith upon the foundation Christ established&mdash;apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/nt/eph/2?lang=eng&id=p19-p20#p19" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ephesians 2:19–20</a>). We must fix our eyes on Jesus, the author and finisher of our faith. The institution is not the foundation. The institution is the household standing upon the foundation, and the household is built of cracked stones, and the building has needed repair in every generation.</p>
        <p class="study-prose" id="flee-harm-not-fellowship">When religious leaders or fellow worshipers shock or disappoint us, we must resist the temptation to walk alone. Though we ought to turn from those that would harm us, we have an apostolic command not to forsake the assembling of ourselves together (Hebrews 10:25). The wounded disciple is not commanded to return to the place of the wound, but he is invited to remain in fellowship with the body of Christ wherever that body can be safely found. The fleeing of harm is not the same as the fleeing of fellowship. That is why disciples who have been wounded can still say, with the apostle{% if aoidSecondEdition.on %} and with the Articles{% endif %}, that we do not forsake the assembling of ourselves together.</p>
        <p class="study-prose">The institution carries the treasure; it is not the treasure. The treasure is Christ. The leadership within any institution is the cracked vessel through which Christ has chosen, in His mercy and according to His own purposes, to reach the souls of God's children.<sup><a href="#addendum-gate" class="study-footnote-marker">&para;</a></sup></p>
        <p class="study-prose"><em>&para; See addendum below for the evidentiary case across the polity spectrum.</em></p>
    </div>
</section>

